# caddy resource: agent guide

Caddy plus a `resource` handler: one definition file per resource (JSON or TypeScript) in a directory becomes a REST endpoint over PostgreSQL: `GET <path>` (paginated), `GET <path>/<id>`, `POST`, `PUT`/`PATCH`/`DELETE <path>/<id>`; JSON-LD/Hydra, JSON or CSV output; `GET /openapi.json`, `GET /` (entrypoint). Nothing to compile. Read a topic (index below) only when you need it.

## Fastest workflow, no server, no probing

- `caddy resource introspect`: tables, columns, sample rows. Start here instead of guessing the schema.
- `caddy resource sql "<query>"`: run SQL (rolled back unless `--commit`).
- `caddy resource request METHOD PATH [-H 'Name: value'] [-d BODY]`: run a request in-process, no server (rolled back unless `--commit`).
- `caddy resource eval 'EXPR'`: evaluate a script expression offline (`ctx.crypto`, `ctx.time`, `ctx.env`).
- `caddy resource validate`: full report after every edit{{if .MCP}} (also the `validate` MCP tool){{end}}.

Flags `--dir resources --dsn <dsn>` (dsn defaults to `$DATABASE_URL`). Details: topic `commands`.

## Workflow

1. `caddy resource init` scaffolds a working `Caddyfile` and example, or write it (Caddyfile options: topic `server`):

```
:8080 {
	resource {
		dir resources
		dsn postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/dbname
{{- if .MCP}}
		mcp on
{{- end}}
	}
}
```

2. `caddy run > caddy.log 2>&1 &` once. It reloads `resources/` by itself within a second; restart only after editing the `Caddyfile`.
3. Write `resources/Book.json` plus the scripts it references; `curl -s localhost:8080/books`.
4. Errors teach: an invalid definition's paths answer `500` with `code`, `pointer`, `hint` (all errors in `errors`); other paths keep working. Every `hint` ends with `see: caddy resource guide <topic>`.

## Example

`resources/Book.json`: table read and written with generated SQL, declared filters and sorting:

```json
{
  "name": "Book",
  "path": "/books",
  "table": "book",
  "itemsPerPage": 10,
  "properties": {
    "id": {"type": "integer", "identifier": true, "readOnly": true},
    "title": {"type": "string", "required": true},
    "priceCents": {"type": "integer", "column": "price_cents"},
    "published": {"type": "boolean"}
  },
  "parameters": {
    "title": {"in": "query", "filter": "ipartial"},
    "priceCents": {"in": "query", "filter": "range"},
    "order": {"in": "query", "filter": "order", "properties": ["title", "priceCents"]}
  }
}
```

`GET /books?title=dune&priceCents[lte]=2000&order[priceCents]=desc&page=2` filters, sorts (then by `id`), paginates, counts. To hide rows, join or compute, add `"query": "book.query.ts"` (one column per property, named like it; no `LIMIT`/`ORDER BY`/count, the server wraps it):

```ts
export default function query(ctx: QueryContext): Sql {
  return ctx.sql`SELECT id, title, price_cents AS "priceCents", published FROM book WHERE published`;
}
```

## Keys

`name`, `path`, `table` (generated SQL), `query` (script returning the read SQL), `command` (script with `create`/`update`/`remove`), `properties` (output), `input` (write body, if different), `parameters` (filters, sorting, page size, headers), `operations` (dedicated endpoints): `definitions`. Scripts: TypeScript or Starlark, same `ctx` API (`scripts`).

